# What each document says about itself. One row per loaded document: the root element it declares,
# the namespace that element is in, the URN it claims, and, from the assertion roots, who witnessed
# it and when.
#
# A relation that reads a root element
#   This is the first relation in this tree that reads a root element. Every other query reaches
#   past it with the descendant axis, because `//pm:stack/pm:layer` finds a stack whether it sits at
#   the root or one level down inside a composition. That is the right shape for asking about a
#   layer and the wrong shape for asking about a document, which is how `filing.kind` ends up a
#   substring match on a namespace prefix. A prefix is the sender's lexical choice; `local-name()`
#   and `namespace-uri()` are the document's own.
#
# The empty column is the finding
#   Five roots are declared across the two schemas, every `asrt:` one names a witness, and
#   `pm:processModulus` is the only one that does not. A filing says which document it is and then
#   stops. Every fact inside it carries a `pm:Provenance`; the document carries none, so a
#   stipulation and an observation are the same shape to anything reading the tree.
#
# `observedAt` and `ranAt` stay two columns
#   They are the same question under two spellings and `asrt:coverage` asks it under neither, so a
#   reader can see three roots owing a date answer it three different ways. Folding them with a
#   `coalesce` would assert an equivalence no schema states, for the same reason `week` and `semana`
#   stay apart in units/denominators.sqlc.
#
# `evidence` is filed, not inferred
#   Writing this query is what made the gap visible: a column for what each document says about
#   itself, with the one fact that decides whether a number may be quoted missing from it. Matching
#   `A STIPULATION, NOT A FILING` as a substring over the whole serialised body instead is
#   English-only in a repository with a Portuguese edition, and true of any corpus document that
#   merely quotes the sentence.
#
# Why `pm.source` is read raw
#   It is the only relation over the documents' own bodies, and it reads each one's root element,
#   which no other relation here has a reason to read.
#
# The other two places a stipulation announces itself
#   Nothing reads them and nothing should. The directory it sits in is rejected as a source on
#   purpose. And the `notation` carries a `:fixture:` segment that no query touches;
#   `contrato-empresarial` already spells the sibling segment `:declaracao:` in the other edition,
#   so the convention has drifted with nothing leaning on it. A URN is an identifier, not a claim.
-- The five top-level declarations: pm:processModulus and asrt:composition/dependence/coverage/run.
SELECT s.name AS filing,
       x.root, x.ns,
       fi.notation, fi.absent AS notation_absent,
       f.evidence, f.evidence_absent,
       x.witness, x.observed_at, x.ran_at
FROM      pm.source s
CROSS JOIN XMLTABLE(XMLNAMESPACES('https://example.invalid/assertion/1.0' AS asrt),
       '/*' PASSING s.body
       COLUMNS root        text PATH 'local-name(.)',
               ns          text PATH 'namespace-uri(.)',
               witness     text PATH 'asrt:witness',
               observed_at text PATH 'asrt:observedAt',
               ran_at      text PATH 'asrt:ranAt') x
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/notations.sqlc)
) fi ON fi.filing = s.name
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(scope/every_filing.sqlc)
) f ON f.filing = s.name
